INSTRUCTION SET ARCHITECTURE: SOURCES AND UNFULFILLED COVERAGE.

Status: reference/provenance contract and explicit gaps, NOT a completed
self-contained transcription of the full ARMv7-A Thumb instruction set.

The selected abstract target is ARMv7-A executing T32 instructions in
unprivileged application mode. "Thumb-2" denotes the 16-bit and 32-bit
Thumb encoding facilities supported in this architecture, NOT a separate
processor, and NOT a claim that every optional extension is fitted.

PRIMARY ARCHITECTURAL REFERENCE.

The normative reference is:

Arm Limited, ARM Architecture Reference Manual, ARMv7-A and ARMv7-R edition,
document ARM DDI 0406, revision C.d, publication identifier ID040418.

Official document landing:
https://developer.arm.com/documentation/ddi0406/cd

Arm document content (C.d):
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5f8daeb7f86e16515cdb8c4e

ADDITIONAL CROSS-CHECK SOURCE (NOT A SUBSTITUTE FOR DDI 0406C.d).

Arm Limited, Instruction Set Assembly Guide for Armv7 and earlier Arm
architectures, Reference Guide, version 2.0, document
100076_0200_00_en (2019).
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5e7b6a6216d2907d594035c4

In that guide, A1.10 describes PC reads, C1 describes condition flags,
C2.14/C2.19-C2.23 describe branches, C2.41 describes IT,
and C2.154 describes TBB/TBH. This edition includes Armv8 and
some other profile-specific forms. No instruction is admitted to this
ARMv7-A T32 profile merely because this guide mentions it.
The primary ARMv7-A-specific DDI 0406C.d must determine
architecture and encoding validity.

ADDITIONAL 16-BIT THUMB QUICK-CARD CROSS-CHECK.

Arm Limited, "Thumb 16-bit Instruction Set", Quick Reference Card,
which describes Thumb-2 16-bit instructions as well as earlier Thumb.
https://documentation-service.arm.com/static/5ed67dc7ca06a95ce53f9334

The page headed "Thumb 16-bit Instruction Set" that covers load,
store, PUSH, POP, and branch operations distinguishes:
  - LDM with its base excluded (base writeback required);
  - LDM with its base included (base writeback omitted);
  - STM with a base writeback in the narrow encoding;
  - PUSH of low registers with optional LR;
  - POP of low registers with optional PC;
  - direct SP arithmetic scaled by four bytes.
These distinctions are material to the English operation names.
The quick card does not enumerate all wide T32 encodings or
override ARM DDI 0406C.d.

This reference is identified by its document number AND revision. A later
version, a similar ARMv7-M manual, an ARMv8-A AArch32 description, a blog
post, and assembler implementation notes are NOT substitutions without a
recorded compatibility review. A vendor Cortex-A reference manual can
supply implementation and feature facts but does not silently replace the
architecture manual.

SECTIONS THAT MUST INFORM AN EXACT ARCHITECTURE PORT.

A2: application programmers' model, 32-bit architectural registers,
    PC operand reads, condition flags, execution state and extension state.
A3: memory model, endianness, memory attributes, ordering, alignment,
    translation and permissible access.
A4: instruction-set overview and groupings, conditional execution,
    branch/exchange behavior and the distinction between ARM and Thumb.
A6: 16-bit and 32-bit Thumb instruction-decode tables.
A8: individual instruction descriptions: all T encodings, operand
    constraints, architectural pseudocode, condition/flag effects,
    deprecations, UNDEFINED, UNPREDICTABLE and optional features.
The applicable system-architecture and extension portions must be
consulted whenever a physical target grants those operations.

The architecture reference MUST be consulted at the specific instruction
encoding, rather than merely matching an assembler mnemonic. Mnemonics may
denote several encodings, aliases, different flag semantics, different
operand restrictions, or a change of instruction state.

This revision DOES NOT reproduce Arm's copyrighted full instruction
descriptions or claim that short English glosses can replace its formal
pseudocode. English Major C must eventually supply its own independently
verified executable operation semantics or explicit reference binding.

FURTHER AUDIT RECORDS IN THIS REPOSITORY.

"Which instruction is next and how it is found.english" specifies
instruction length, execution index, PC operand and control-flow traces.

"Exactly when execution branches.english" specifies condition flags
and permitted branching mechanisms in long English.

"When bytes mean data and when bytes mean instructions.english"
separates data loads from instruction fetch and records the
alternative execution models considered.

"How registers enter memory and return from a stack.english"
specifies the narrow T32 PUSH, POP, LDMIA, STMIA, and
SP add/subtract operations, their word ordering and
writeback, plus rejected base-overlap and exception
assumptions. The closed initial grant includes
seven additional operations, numbered 54 through 60.

"Actual wide Thumb multiple-word operations in long English.english"
in ../../../examples/ records the additional four-byte STMIA
and LDMDB instructions with W=0 and W=1, admitted
under separately named operations 61 through 64.
Their subset has at least two selected general
registers and explicitly excludes SP/PC and
base-register overlap.

"Exactly how register bits are rearranged.english" defines
new operation entries 65 through 72, covering limited
REV, REV16, REVSH, RBIT, BFC, BFI, UBFX, SBFX forms,
including their register and width restrictions.

"Every halfword in one complete Thumb decode region.english"
closes a finite 256-halfword subtree, BA00..BAFF,
with 192 allocated byte-reversal encodings and 64
reserved/UNDEFINED encodings that MUST NOT be executed
as a permitted operation. Its 256 data rows are under
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-byte-reversal-halfwords.tsv.
That exhaustive subtree proof does not count as exhaustive
coverage of all 16-bit T32 or all 32-bit T32 encodings.

"Every conditional-branch and supervisor-call halfword.english"
defines the ENTIRE 0xD000 through 0xDFFF sixteen-bit Thumb
decoding subtree, using DDI 0406C.d A6.2.6 Table A6-8
and A8.8.18 for conditional B T1. The 4096-row table:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-and-exception-halfwords.tsv
has 3584 condition branches, 256 permanently undefined
UDF encodings and 256 supervisor-call SVC encodings.
Only the B T1 class is granted, under operation 43, and
it is NOT permitted inside an IT block.

The complete fourteen-condition by sixteen-flag-state
truth table (224 rows) is:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-conditional-branch-flag-truth-table.tsv
This evaluates conditions over abstract N/Z/C/V flag
values; it is not an executed machine-state trace.

"A conditional branch chooses which exceptional instruction is next.english"
in ../../../examples/ uses inspected T32 bytes and two
declared machine states to show one trace that skips
UDF and halts at ungranted SVC, and another that
halts at ungranted UDF. ARM UDF and SVC are distinct
exception mechanisms, not short condition branches.

"Every If Then and hint halfword.english" describes the
complete BF00..BFFF 256-halfword subtree under
ARM DDI 0406C.d A6.2.5 Table A6-7 and A8.8.55.
Its full data table is:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-if-then-and-hint-halfwords.tsv
The subtree includes 214 conditionally admissible
IT forms; 26 IT encodings rejected as UNPREDICTABLE;
one exact permitted NOP, four named but ungranted
hints, and eleven software-reserved unallocated hints.
It does NOT mean that the full ITSTATE execution
interpreter or every following-instruction context
has already been implemented.

"One If Then block governs a short and a long instruction.english"
in ../../../examples/ translates a foreign diagnostic
of an actual 16-bit IT followed by a two-byte MOV
and a four-byte MOVW, with different register effects
depending on the initial comparison flags, but no
different execution-index sequence. Its diagnostic
source was assembled and decoded, not run on ARM hardware.

"2026-10-11-BFxx-Thumb-IT-and-hint-audit.english"
in ../../../evidence/ captures its temporary
clang/LLVM diagnostic, the difference between
LLVM's apparently legal printed IT and the ARM
UNPREDICTABLE condition, SHA256 hashes and blockers.

"Every short unconditional branch halfword.english"
specifies the full E000..E7FF T32 B T2 encoding
family, 2048 distinct two-byte halfwords, each
with signed eleven-bit PC-relative displacement
and precise last-slot IT restrictions. The
row-by-row table is:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-unconditional-branch-halfwords.tsv
All allocated forms map to EXISTING operation 42,
under their legal source and target context.
The word "unconditional" names an encoding without
a condition field; ITSTATE may legally predicate
its BRANCH EFFECT in the final IT slot.

"Every breakpoint halfword and its
conditional-execution exception.english"
specifies the complete BE00..BEFF region:
256 T32 BKPT T1 halfwords with unsigned
immediates, all decoded but UNGRANTED.
Their data-only inventory is:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-breakpoint-halfwords.tsv
Unlike an ordinary instruction governed by IT,
BKPT remains always unconditional, EVEN when
the surrounding IT predicate is false.
A real debug-event outcome depends on target
debug settings and OS exception behavior,
and cannot be inferred from disassembly alone.

"The same branch bytes acquire a condition from
If Then state.english" in ../../../examples/
retains a diagnostic E000 which LLVM displays
as B outside IT but BEQ after IT EQ.
It shows two distinct source-level register
and execution-index traces that end at an
ungranted SVC boundary.

"2026-10-11-E000-and-BE00-Thumb-decode-audit.english"
under ../../../evidence/ preserves the temporary
clang/LLVM tool versions, source/object/disassembly
digests, all 2048 branch/displacement checks,
256 BKPT decode checks, and exact evidence limits.

Together the five disjoint decoder subtrees cover
  BA00..BAFF: 256 values
  BE00..BEFF: 256 values
  BF00..BFFF: 256 values
  D000..DFFF: 4096 values
  E000..E7FF: 2048 values
or 6912 individual 16-bit halfword values at
decoder-classification level. The separate
initial executable vocabulary at that earlier stage had 72
long English operation names. These checks
do NOT cover the full 16-bit or 32-bit Thumb
ISA, complete ITSTATE and debug exceptions,
or actual processor/OS acceptance.

"Eight Thumb bit operations translated until the first ungranted
instruction.english" in ../../../examples/ contains
an actual foreign-assembler 16/32-bit byte listing, an
English execution-index and register trace, and an explicit
stop at the first ungranted TEQ instruction. It is
diagnostic source evidence, NOT a qualified ICK build.

"Actual Thumb word transfers translated into English.english"
in ../../../examples/ records independently sourced
AOSP halfwords C409, CC09, B014, and B094 and
their separately stated machine-state transitions.

"What the current instruction grant does not yet contain.english"
holds the outstanding instruction-family inventory, including
ungranted wide PUSH/POP and LDM/STM forms, barriers,
exclusives, floating-point, SIMD and optional processor
extensions. Restricted 16-bit stack and multiple-word
forms are separately permitted by operations 54-60.

"One actual Thumb branch listing translated from the bottom upward.english"
in ../../../examples/ gives a source-derived machine-byte specimen
and exposes the distinct MOV versus MOVS flag behavior.

"Every halfword that forms an address from the execution position
or stack pointer.english" declares the COMPLETE finite 4096-halfword
A000..AFFF subtree. It has 2048 T32 ADR T1 forms using
Align(PC+4,4) plus the unsigned immediate times four, and 2048
T32 ADD (SP plus immediate) T1 forms producing a new
low-register value without modifying SP. Existing operation 40
is explicitly limited to the ADR T1 encoding; the latter
SP-relative register-producing form is separately named
new long operation 73. The complete data table is:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-address-generation-halfwords.tsv

"Every PC-relative data-word load halfword.english" declares
the COMPLETE 2048-halfword 4800..4FFF T32 LDR literal T1
subtree. It calculates Align(PC+4,4)+4*imm8 and then performs
an actual FOUR-BYTE DATA-MEMORY READ, subject to mapping
and access permissions. Existing operation 41 is now
limited to that exact narrow T1 form. Its data rows are:
../../../fixtures/armv7-a-t32-pc-relative-word-load-halfwords.tsv

"An address a load and a branch disagree about the same bytes.english"
in ../../../examples/ preserves a REAL assembled 16-byte
ELF32 ARM .text section and the precise code/data ambiguity:
its literal bytes 78 56 34 12 are one declared DATA WORD,
which a linear LLVM disassembler nonetheless prints as
two potential instruction encodings. The actual branch
skips their addresses; a preceding LDR reads their word
value; an ADR merely calculates a numerical address.
The language trace stops before an ungranted SVC.

"2026-10-11-PC-relative-address-and-data-load-audit.english"
in ../../../evidence/ records actual foreign compiler/decoder
diagnostics of all 6144 newly classified halfwords,
observed artifact SHA256 digests and the precise
limits of arithmetic and memory-model verification.

The earlier FIVE disjoint subtrees classified 6912
numerical two-byte Thumb halfword values. The added
4096 Axxx and 2048 48xx..4Fxx forms are disjoint from
those five, giving exactly SEVEN fully enumerated
decoder-classification subtrees containing
13056 DISTINCT numerical sixteen-bit halfword values.
This is NOT completeness of the ARMv7-A T32 ISA,
even of every instruction form underlying the 73
currently permitted long English operations.
The verified target processor and runtime remain unspecified.

ENGLISH MAJOR C PROFILE BOUNDARY.

The sole initial operation grant is:
    All and only these initial operations are permitted.english

It lists 73 named families. No machine instruction outside a verified
lowering of one of those named families is thereby permitted by the
language. That is a complete statement of the INITIAL SOFTWARE GRANT,
but it is NOT a complete statement of all the instructions executable
by a physical ARMv7-A processor.

"All and only" is therefore a quantifier over the selected named
operation profile. It does not mean that every architecturally possible
physical instruction is present in that initial profile.

WHAT HAS NOT BEEN ENUMERATED OR VERIFIED.

The open coverage inventory includes, without implying exhaustiveness:

  * every T32 encoding variant, width, restriction and alias even for
    the 73 named operation families;
  * full multiply, multiply-accumulate, dual-multiply, long-multiply
    and signed/unsigned divide families;
  * saturating, parallel arithmetic, packing, byte reversal,
    bit-field selection and miscellaneous integer operations;
  * all memory-addressing variants, register-list transfers,
    stack aliases, exclusives, synchronization and memory barriers;
  * exception/supervisor, CPSR/SPSR and mode-control facilities,
    system access, coprocessor and privileged instructions;
  * all architectural hints, instructions that may be deprecated,
    unavailable, unconditional-encoding special cases and traps;
  * VFP / floating-point support, Advanced SIMD / NEON and optional
    processor-specific architecture extensions;
  * ThumbEE and Jazelle, where relevant, which are not granted here;
  * proof that instruction selection is identical across actual
    processors with different supported architecture features;
  * target operating-system ABI, toolchain, virtual-memory permissions,
    exception handling, calling convention, code generation and
    actual execution.

Some of these instruction classes are wholly absent from the initial
allowed vocabulary by design. Others will be subject to a future
coverage or exact-encoding audit. The reference manual is the
canonical source against which any statement of hardware-wide
instruction completeness MUST be reconciled.

FULL-CATALOGUE COMPLETION CRITERION.

Before anyone labels an English Major C file "the complete ARMv7-A T32
instruction set", an independent review SHALL:

1. Pin and identify the architectural revision and target feature profile.
2. Enumerate every T32 decode-table leaf for that feature profile,
   including valid encodings, conditional forms, aliases, reserved
   encodings and legal instructions only when their conditions hold.
3. Associate each reachable valid form with a unique or explicitly
   shared long English operation name and the full input/output/effect
   specification, including PC, flags, ITSTATE, memory, exceptions,
   processor state, and any required privilege.
4. Demonstrate how two-byte and four-byte fetch/decode boundaries,
   state interworking, endian choices and unaligned accesses are handled.
5. Reject every intentionally invalid, missing-feature, forbidden,
   implementation-dependent-without-manifest, or unpredicted form.
6. Audit instruction groups and count against the ARM reference;
   record discrepancies rather than assuming a mnemonic index complete.
7. Compile/encode/decode checked fixtures only using an authorized
   toolchain, and validate results independently. Report source,
   encode/decode, semantic execution and hardware evidence separately.

Until then, do not describe the 73-entry grant as the entire physical ISA.

TARGET-MACHINE DECLARATION REQUIREMENT.

The first future concrete declaration must identify the actual SoC, CPU
core/revision, supported feature bits, architectural execution state,
user-mode environment, endianness, memory permissions, permitted
instruction forms, relevant cache coherency protocol and ABI. No
unverified RAM/DRAM part number, device register map, or physical channel
address shall be invented. A CPU architectural instruction cannot
be promoted from "supported in a family" to "observed executable on
this device" merely from the instruction-set name.

